We thank the reviewer for pointing out the lack of clarity in the terminology definitions. In the revised manuscript, we have provided explicit definitions for all relevant terms in Section 3.2 “Supervised Fine-Tuning.”

For the Stage 1 loss components, we clearly define the following:
$\mathcal{L}\_{\text{LM}}$ denotes the standard autoregressive next-token prediction loss;
$\mathcal{L}\_{\text{align}}$ represents the cross-modal alignment loss, which encourages consistent representations between paired visual and textual inputs through cosine similarity;
$\mathcal{L}\_{\text{modal}}$ captures modality consistency by minimizing the divergence between the outputs of the vision encoder and the language encoder.

For the Stage 2 notation, we specify that
$\mathbf{q} \in \mathbb{R}^d$ is the query representation vector obtained by mean-pooling the hidden states from the query encoder, and
$\mathbf{c}\_i \in \mathbb{R}^d$ denotes the contextualized embedding of the $i$-th retrieved document, where $i \in {1, \dots, K}$ and we retrieve $K = 5$ documents for each query.

For the Stage 2 loss components, we clearly define the following:
$\mathcal{L}\_{\text{retrieval}}$ measures the model’s attention allocation to relevant retrieved documents using binary cross-entropy;
$\mathcal{L}\_{\text{consistency}}$ enforces factual consistency between the generated content and retrieved context using the BERTScore F1 metric with a threshold of 0.85;
$\mathcal{L}\_{\text{relevance}}$ applies pairwise margin ranking loss with the margin parameter set to $\gamma = 0.1$.

These additional definitions make our methodological description more complete and reproducible. We again thank the reviewer for their attention to technical precision and clarity.
